    "id": 239,
    "project_title": "Fintech Hub",
    "project_tags": null,
    "project_location": "GIFT City, Gujarat, India",
    "project_site_area": "53,843 SF",
    "project_builtup_area": "8,66,180 SF",
    "project_services": "Architecture, Interior Design, Landscape Design, Engineering, Sustainability",
    "project_quote": "A deconstructed architecture of solids, voids, and landscapes that transforms a corporate tower into a breathable environment for climate, collaboration, and community.",
    "project_team": null,
    "project_collaboration": null,
    "project_summary_1": "<strong>Deconstructing the Conventional Corporate Tower</strong><br>Situated within GIFT City's emerging financial district, the Fintech Hub reimagines the contemporary corporate tower through the lens of vertical urbanism, transforming a singular office block into an environment where architecture, landscape, climate, and human interaction are woven together. Rather than a singular, monolithic volume, the tower is conceived as a composition of interlocking geometries that are strategically carved, shifted, and opened to create a dynamic three-dimensional workplace ecosystem.",
    "project_summary_2": "Its mass is broken into a series of angular volumes that generate dramatic cantilevers, recessed terraces, and open-air voids, transforming a traditional office block\r\ninto a dynamic, breathable sculpture that carves a distinctive identity into the city’s neutral skyline. Here, architecture is conceived as a continuously evolving spatial landscape that accommodates movement, interaction, and occupation at multiple scales.",
    "project_summary_3": "<strong>Carving Solids and Voids to Create Communities</strong><br>The architecture is generated through a process of subtraction rather than addition. Angular carve-outs, recessed volumes, and dramatic cantilevers break down the overall mass into a hierarchy of interconnected spaces that mediate between built form and open air. These voids are not residual spaces but active environments that accommodate terraces, stepped plazas, and shared communal spaces throughout the tower. By fragmenting the mass rather than stacking repetitive floor plates, the building creates opportunities for interaction, visual connectivity, and moments of pause within a dense corporate setting.",
